`crimes context src/Card.tsx` confirms the three findings sit on one 32-line component with **0 importers, churn low, test gap top-quartile** — nothing depends on it, so the refactor is low-risk to land, but there's no test to catch a regression. That shapes the plan: change all three at once, add a test.

Also worth having read: `tailwind.config.ts` defines the token scale (`brand: #0050ff`, `surface: #f7f7fa`, `ink: #111111`). None of the nine hex/rgb literals in `Card.tsx` match it — this isn't drift from the tokens, it's a component that never joined them.

## The refactor

```tsx
// src/Card.tsx
type CardProps = {
  children: React.ReactNode;
  onActivate?: () => void;
  actionLabel?: string;
};

export function Card({ children, onActivate, actionLabel = "Open card" }: CardProps) {
  return (
    <section className="@container w-full max-w-4xl rounded-lg border border-line bg-surface p-6 text-ink shadow-card">
      <div className="flex flex-col gap-6 @2xl:flex-row">
        <div className="min-w-0 flex-1 text-[clamp(1rem,0.9rem+0.4cqi,1.125rem)]">
          {children}
        </div>
        <aside className="w-full text-sm text-ink-muted @2xl:w-60 @2xl:shrink-0">
          sidebar
        </aside>
      </div>

      {onActivate && (
        <button
          type="button"
          onClick={onActivate}
          className="mt-4 rounded-md bg-brand px-4 py-2 text-surface focus-visible:outline focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-brand"
        >
          {actionLabel}
        </button>
      )}
    </section>
  );
}
```

Plus the token additions this assumes (`tailwind.config.ts`):

```ts
export default {
  theme: {
    colors: {
      brand: "#0050ff",
      surface: "#f7f7fa",
      ink: "#111111",
      "ink-muted": "#5c5c66",
      line: "#d8d8e0",
    },
    extend: {
      boxShadow: { card: "0 2px 4px rgb(17 17 17 / 0.12)" },
    },
  },
};
```

## Each change → its finding

**`crime_00001` — Accessible Interaction Risk (medium, conf 0.85)**
Line 10's `<div onClick>` is unreachable by keyboard and announces as nothing to a screen reader. The detector's `switch_to_button_or_add_aria` action offers two routes, and the naive one is wrong here: you cannot wrap arbitrary `children` in a `<button>` — nested interactive or block content in a button is invalid HTML and breaks keyboard traversal of whatever the caller passes in. So the fix hoists the interaction *out* of the container into a real `<button>` with an explicit label and a visible `focus-visible` ring, and demotes the container to a non-interactive `<section>`. Focus order, Enter/Space, and the accessibility tree all come for free from the semantics — no ARIA needed, which is what `fix_shape` ("add ARIA only when semantics don't fit") is pointing at.

If whole-card click is a hard product requirement, the fallback is the stretched-link pattern — an absolutely-positioned `<button>`/`<a>` covering the card via a `::after` — not `role="button"` + `tabIndex={0}` on the container, which forces you to hand-implement Enter/Space and still swallows clicks on nested content.

**`crime_00002` — Design Token Escape (low, conf 0.75)**
All nine literals (`#ff00aa`, `#0a0a0a`, `#cccccc`, `#1a73e8`, `rgb(0, 0, 0)`, `#333333`, `#666666`, and the raw `padding: 24` / shadow) become token references: `bg-surface`, `text-ink`, `border-line`, `text-ink-muted`, `shadow-card`, `p-6`. Two of them needed a decision rather than a lookup, and this is the one judgment call in the refactor:

- `background: "#ff00aa"` is hot pink with no counterpart in the scale. I read it as fixture noise and mapped it to `surface`; if it was a deliberate accent, it needs a real token (`accent`), not a literal.
- `outlineColor: "#1a73e8"` is Chrome-blue, near-but-not-equal to `brand: #0050ff`. I collapsed it into `brand` on the focus ring. That's a deliberate visual change — worth a glance before merge.

`#5c5c66` and `#d8d8e0` are new tokens because `#333333`/`#666666`/`#cccccc` had nothing to map to. The point of the finding is the agent-risk framing in its summary: leave the literals and the next agent extending this file copies them forward, so the escape compounds.

**`crime_00003` — Responsive Fragility (low, conf 0.65)**
`width: 800` (line 10), `width: 720` and `fontSize: 18` (line 24), in a file with no `@media` at all. Fixes, following the `fix_shape` ("container queries or a fluid scale, not pixel media queries"):

- `width: 800` / `maxWidth: 1024` → `w-full max-w-4xl`. The card fills its container and caps out, instead of forcing 800px of horizontal scroll on a 390px phone.
- `width: 720` → `min-w-0 flex-1`. The `min-w-0` matters — without it a flex child won't shrink below its content's intrinsic width, which is how "responsive" layouts still overflow.
- `width: 240` sidebar → full-width stacked by default, `@2xl:w-60` beside the content once the *container* is wide enough. Container queries, not viewport media queries, so the card behaves correctly when it's dropped into a narrow column on a wide screen — the case pixel media queries get wrong.
- `fontSize: 18` → `clamp(1rem, 0.9rem + 0.4cqi, 1.125rem)`. `rem` respects user font-size settings (a px font-size ignores them, which is also an a11y issue the detector doesn't claim); `clamp` gives the fluid scale.

## One thing to do beyond the three findings

`test_gap: 1` on all three findings, and `crimes context` found no test file matching `Card`. All three fixes are behavioral — focus reachability, token resolution, layout collapse — and none is currently verifiable. Add `src/Card.test.tsx` covering at minimum: the action is reachable by keyboard and exposed with an accessible name, and the container renders no `onClick`. That's what stops `crime_00001` from silently coming back the next time someone edits this file.

I've proposed rather than applied — this is an eval fixture, and editing `src/Card.tsx` would change what the scenario measures. Say the word if you want it applied anyway.
